Description: Tools to solve Maximal Coverage Location-Allocation problems
    with geospatial data, i.e., to place new facilities so that as much
    (optionally weighted) demand as possible can reach a facility within a
    given travel time. Travel times are computed over the friction surfaces of
    the Malaria Atlas Project (Weiss et al., 2018 <doi:10.1038/nature25181>),
    optionally refined with OpenStreetMap road networks. New facilities can be
    placed anywhere within an area (continuous problem) or selected among
    candidate locations (discrete problem), using greedy (Church and ReVelle,
    1974 <doi:10.1007/BF01942293>) and vertex substitution (Teitz and Bart,
    1968 <doi:10.1287/opre.16.5.955>) heuristics. It is designed to help
    decision-makers allocate resources and facilities in contexts such as
    public health, emergency response, climate adaptation, and urban
    planning.
